Headline: League table of Drivers: who covers the most distance, who deviates from plan, and who accumulates the most risk events.
Business question: Which Drivers need coaching, and which ones are our benchmark?
Audience: Fleet safety manager; Depot or branch manager; Training lead; Insurance and risk.
Industries: Logistics and transport; Field service; Passenger transport; Public sector fleets.
Talk track: Start at the KPI row: Driver count, total Trips, average distance, deviated Trips and safety events.; Show the leaderboard sorted by safety events descending and frame it as the coaching list.; Use the factor chart to name which kind of event dominates.; Click an Driver row and show their activity on the map.; Contrast the top and the bottom of the table, and land it as a coaching conversation rather than a ranking for its own sake..
Snowflake capabilities: Per-operator aggregation over trip and event data, with masking and row access policies available on the same tables; Semantic view (SV_FLEET_OPS) so the agent answers distance and trip-count rankings without new SQL; Telemetry-derived events combined with operational trip data in a single query.
Data required: Operator master, assigned to vehicles or trips; Trip records with distance and duration; Safety or exception events attributed to an operator or trip.
Value drivers: Target coaching where it changes claims and fuel cost, instead of training everyone equally; Evidence for insurance and risk negotiations; Recognise the good operators on the same evidence used to correct the others.
Method: Trip counts, distance and speed come from the trip records. Per-operator safety-event counts come from the event data and are not part of the semantic view, so they are event-derived rather than modelled metrics. The leaderboard is sorted by safety events descending.
Caveats: Synthetic operators and events. In a real deployment, whether per-person ranking is acceptable and who may see it is a decision for the customer and their works council; the platform supports masking and row access policies for exactly that reason.
Key metrics on screen: operator (driver) count; total journeys; average trip distance; deviated journeys; safety events per operator.
Example questions: Who has the most safety events? Which operator covers the most distance? How does operator X compare to operator Y? What is the safety event rate per journey?
Gotchas: query_fleet_ops (SV_FLEET_OPS) covers operator trip counts, distance, speed, shift, and driver profile. The per-operator safety-event counts in the table and chart come from the safety event data, which is NOT in the semantic view; quote those from the on-screen metric values or state they are event-derived rather than implying the tool can rank them. The leaderboard is sorted by safety events descending.
Preferred analytics tool: query_fleet_ops.$$
