SELECT
  $$safety_risk_scorecard$$, $$Safety / Risk Scorecard$$, $$Core$$,
  $$Operational risk by severity - click a KPI tile to filter, review the event queue, and map hot spots.$$,
  $$Operational risk on one screen: event volume by severity, the behaviour mix behind it, and where the hot spots are.$$,
  $$Where is our risk concentrated, and what would we fix first to reduce claims?$$,
  ARRAY_CONSTRUCT($$Safety manager$$, $$Risk and insurance$$, $$Compliance officer$$, $$Operations director$$),
  ARRAY_CONSTRUCT($$Logistics and transport$$, $$Passenger transport$$, $$Utilities$$, $$Public sector fleets$$),
  ARRAY_CONSTRUCT($$Start with the severity tiles and click High to filter the whole page to high-severity events.$$, $$Read the event-type mix to name the behaviour worth targeting.$$, $$Work the event queue as a triage list.$$, $$Show the map hot spots, and point out that some are infrastructure rather than driver behaviour.$$, $$Close on the link to Driver Performance for the coaching list.$$),
  ARRAY_CONSTRUCT($$Event detection over raw telemetry inside Snowflake, with no separate rules engine to run$$, $$Click-to-filter analytics where the KPI tile is itself the filter control$$, $$Governed access to sensitive behaviour data using masking and row access policies on the same tables$$),
  ARRAY_CONSTRUCT($$Safety or exception events with severity, type, time and location$$, $$Vehicle and operator master, to attribute events$$, $$Optional: claims data, to correlate events with actual loss$$),
  ARRAY_CONSTRUCT($$Reduce claim frequency by targeting the few behaviours and locations that drive it$$, $$Evidence for a premium negotiation$$, $$Separate driver-behaviour risk from road and site risk, so the fix matches the cause$$),
  $$Events carry a severity and a type and are aggregated by both. The KPI tiles double as the filter, so the queue, chart and map always reflect the selected severity.$$,
  $$Synthetic events, and they are not modelled in any semantic view. The assistant therefore answers from the on-screen values rather than recomputing them, and will not invent per-operator trends or event types.$$,
  $$$$,
  ARRAY_CONSTRUCT($$total safety / risk events$$, $$high, medium, and low severity counts$$, $$event types (speeding, harsh braking, and similar)$$),
  ARRAY_CONSTRUCT($$How many high-severity events are there?$$, $$How do high, medium, and low counts compare?$$, $$What severity filter is active?$$),
  $$Safety / Risk Scorecard (Core domain, view id: safety_risk_scorecard).
Headline: Operational risk on one screen: event volume by severity, the behaviour mix behind it, and where the hot spots are.
Business question: Where is our risk concentrated, and what would we fix first to reduce claims?
Audience: Safety manager; Risk and insurance; Compliance officer; Operations director.
Industries: Logistics and transport; Passenger transport; Utilities; Public sector fleets.
Talk track: Start with the severity tiles and click High to filter the whole page to high-severity events.; Read the event-type mix to name the behaviour worth targeting.; Work the event queue as a triage list.; Show the map hot spots, and point out that some are infrastructure rather than driver behaviour.; Close on the link to Driver Performance for the coaching list..
Snowflake capabilities: Event detection over raw telemetry inside Snowflake, with no separate rules engine to run; Click-to-filter analytics where the KPI tile is itself the filter control; Governed access to sensitive behaviour data using masking and row access policies on the same tables.
Data required: Safety or exception events with severity, type, time and location; Vehicle and operator master, to attribute events; Optional: claims data, to correlate events with actual loss.
Value drivers: Reduce claim frequency by targeting the few behaviours and locations that drive it; Evidence for a premium negotiation; Separate driver-behaviour risk from road and site risk, so the fix matches the cause.
Method: Events carry a severity and a type and are aggregated by both. The KPI tiles double as the filter, so the queue, chart and map always reflect the selected severity.
Caveats: Synthetic events, and they are not modelled in any semantic view. The assistant therefore answers from the on-screen values rather than recomputing them, and will not invent per-operator trends or event types.
Key metrics on screen: total safety / risk events; high, medium, and low severity counts; event types (speeding, harsh braking, and similar).
Example questions: How many high-severity events are there? How do high, medium, and low counts compare? What severity filter is active?
Gotchas: Safety events (severity, event type, per-driver counts) live in the scoped event data and are NOT modeled in any semantic view, so there is no Cortex Analyst tool that can recompute them. Answer count and severity-breakdown questions from the on-screen metric values (total events and high/medium/low counts published to panel context) and never invent event types, per-driver counts, or trends. The filters selected_severity and selected_entity show the active tile and row. A dedicated safety-event semantic view would be required to answer event-type rankings or trends via a tool.$$
