SELECT
  $$space_time_density$$, $$Space-Time Density$$, $$Core$$,
  $$Where activity concentrates over space and time - H3 hexbins by hour (press play to animate). Merges Congestion Map and Demand Density.$$,
  $$Where and when activity concentrates: an H3 hexagon heatmap you can animate hour by hour.$$,
  $$Where does our activity cluster, and at what times of day, so depots, shifts and capacity can be placed against real demand?$$,
  ARRAY_CONSTRUCT($$Network planner$$, $$Operations strategy$$, $$Depot siting$$, $$Demand planner$$),
  ARRAY_CONSTRUCT($$Last-mile delivery$$, $$Ride hail and mobility$$, $$Field service$$, $$Retail logistics$$),
  ARRAY_CONSTRUCT($$Choose the metric: time spent, or distinct Vehicles per hexagon.$$, $$Press play on the hour slider and let the day sweep, so the peak windows become obvious.$$, $$Turn off aggregate-all to compare a single hour against the whole day.$$, $$Read the top places and top Drivers tables under the map.$$, $$Land the point: this is where capacity should be, not where it happens to be today.$$),
  ARRAY_CONSTRUCT($$Native H3 aggregation (H3_POINT_TO_CELL_STRING) over millions of pings, with no PostGIS and no tile server$$, $$Time-sliced spatial aggregation computed on demand rather than pre-baked per hour$$, $$GEOGRAPHY-first schema feeding deck.gl directly from SQL$$),
  ARRAY_CONSTRUCT($$Position pings with timestamp and location$$, $$Optional: place or site master, to label the top locations$$),
  ARRAY_CONSTRUCT($$Site depots, lockers and charge points against observed demand rather than intuition$$, $$Shape shift patterns around the hours that actually carry the load$$, $$Spot congestion and dead zones in the network you already run$$),
  $$Pings are bucketed into H3 cells and by hour of day. The metric selector switches between time spent and distinct Vehicles per cell; the hour slider filters to one hour and aggregate-all sums the whole day.$$,
  $$Synthetic telemetry, so the clusters reflect the generator's route pool rather than a real market. Time spent per cell is derived from ping cadence, so an area sampled more often reads hotter.$$,
  $$query_fleet_ops$$,
  ARRAY_CONSTRUCT($$activity density by H3 hex$$, $$time spent (dwell minutes) per hex$$, $$distinct vehicles per hex$$, $$top places and drivers by dwell$$),
  ARRAY_CONSTRUCT($$Where does fleet activity concentrate?$$, $$What are the top places by dwell?$$, $$Which hour of day is busiest?$$),
  $$Space-Time Density (Core domain, view id: space_time_density).
Headline: Where and when activity concentrates: an H3 hexagon heatmap you can animate hour by hour.
Business question: Where does our activity cluster, and at what times of day, so depots, shifts and capacity can be placed against real demand?
Audience: Network planner; Operations strategy; Depot siting; Demand planner.
Industries: Last-mile delivery; Ride hail and mobility; Field service; Retail logistics.
Talk track: Choose the metric: time spent, or distinct Vehicles per hexagon.; Press play on the hour slider and let the day sweep, so the peak windows become obvious.; Turn off aggregate-all to compare a single hour against the whole day.; Read the top places and top Drivers tables under the map.; Land the point: this is where capacity should be, not where it happens to be today..
Snowflake capabilities: Native H3 aggregation (H3_POINT_TO_CELL_STRING) over millions of pings, with no PostGIS and no tile server; Time-sliced spatial aggregation computed on demand rather than pre-baked per hour; GEOGRAPHY-first schema feeding deck.gl directly from SQL.
Data required: Position pings with timestamp and location; Optional: place or site master, to label the top locations.
Value drivers: Site depots, lockers and charge points against observed demand rather than intuition; Shape shift patterns around the hours that actually carry the load; Spot congestion and dead zones in the network you already run.
Method: Pings are bucketed into H3 cells and by hour of day. The metric selector switches between time spent and distinct Vehicles per cell; the hour slider filters to one hour and aggregate-all sums the whole day.
Caveats: Synthetic telemetry, so the clusters reflect the generator's route pool rather than a real market. Time spent per cell is derived from ping cadence, so an area sampled more often reads hotter.
Key metrics on screen: activity density by H3 hex; time spent (dwell minutes) per hex; distinct vehicles per hex; top places and drivers by dwell.
Example questions: Where does fleet activity concentrate? What are the top places by dwell? Which hour of day is busiest?
Gotchas: The H3 hex heatmap and the hour-of-day animation are computed client-side from vehicle telemetry and are not in a semantic view. query_fleet_ops answers trip and origin volume aggregates, not per-hex or per-hour density. The filters selected_metric (time or entities), selected_hour, and aggregate_all describe what is currently rendered on the map.
Preferred analytics tool: query_fleet_ops.$$
