Snowflake capabilities: Live drive-time isochrones for surviving sites tested against pre-rasterized H3 household cells, so coverage is computed rather than eyeballed; Nearest-surviving-store reassignment computed in SQL at interaction time; A finance model whose assumptions are sliders on screen, recomputed live.
Data required: Store estate with locations; Household or address density; Your own value per household, retention rate and EBITDA margin; A provisioned routing region covering the estate.
Value drivers: Close the sites whose demand is genuinely absorbed, rather than the ones with the worst P&L; Quantify the leakage before it shows up in next year's revenue; Prepare the transfer plan for the stores that inherit the volume.
Method: The map shows a household H3 hexagon heatmap of the closing store's drive-time catchment, shaded by how many surviving stores still reach each cell (red = at risk or thin coverage, green = well covered). Coverage is computed from pre-rasterized H3 res-8 household cells tested against live drive-time isochrones, not from stacked polygons. A postcode (ZIP) is RETAINED if at least one surviving store's catchment reaches its centroid within the band, otherwise AT_RISK, and its revenue is the closure leakage. Revenue and EBITDA are user-driven: households x value per household x retention rate, with EBITDA = revenue x margin, and the Home Visit / Sample / Walk-in split comes from the closing store's interaction mix. Closure risk score = at-risk revenue / total closing-store revenue. The gainers table assigns each catchment cell to its nearest surviving store (non-overlapping).
Caveats: Households are real address density, but revenue, EBITDA and the retained-versus-at-risk split all depend on the assumptions set on screen. Overlap-summary rows are pairwise (closed store versus one surviving store), so do not sum households across rows, because surviving-store catchments themselves overlap. The store estate and its interaction mix are synthetic, and customer loyalty to a specific site is not modelled beyond the retention rate.
Key metrics on screen: revenue retained by surviving stores; revenue at risk; closure risk score; households reassigned; households at risk.
Example questions: If we close this store, which store inherits the most sales? How much revenue is at risk if we close this store? What is the closure risk score for this store?
Gotchas: A postcode (ZIP) is RETAINED if at least one surviving store's drive-time catchment reaches its centroid within the band, otherwise AT_RISK (its revenue is the closure leakage). Revenue and EBITDA are derived from the user-supplied value per household, retention rate and EBITDA margin; the Home Visit / Sample / Walk-in split comes from the closing store's interaction mix. Closure risk score = at-risk revenue / total closing-store revenue. Overlap rows are pairwise (closed store vs one surviving store); do not sum households across rows because surviving catchments overlap.
