SELECT
  $$mix_sourcing$$, $$Product Mix Sourcing$$, $$Location$$,
  $$For each customer's mixed-product order, compare shipping every product from its own cheapest plant (ship direct) against transferring the missing products into one hub plant and shipping a single consolidated truckload (consolidate). Shows the freight saving and the handling penalty of consolidating.$$,
  $$For a multi-product order, decide between shipping direct from several plants or consolidating into one truckload.$$,
  $$For customers buying several products, is it cheaper to ship from each plant or to transfer everything into one hub and send a single load?$$,
  ARRAY_CONSTRUCT($$Supply chain planner$$, $$Logistics manager$$, $$Order management$$, $$CFO$$),
  ARRAY_CONSTRUCT($$Building materials$$, $$Glass and packaging$$, $$Chemicals$$, $$Industrial distribution$$),
  ARRAY_CONSTRUCT($$Read the header: total consolidation savings, orders where consolidation wins, orders better shipped direct, and the handling cost paid to consolidate.$$, $$Open an order where consolidation wins and walk the two options in the drawer.$$, $$Show the map: the transfer legs into the hub, then one outbound load.$$, $$Raise the handling slider until consolidation stops winning - that boundary is the customer's real decision rule.$$, $$Point out a single-line order showing near-zero saving, because there is nothing to consolidate.$$),
  ARRAY_CONSTRUCT($$Two live ORS MATRIX_TABULAR calls covering plant-to-customer and plant-to-plant road distance, computed on demand$$, $$Two complete fulfilment options costed per order in SQL$$, $$Interactive rate and handling assumptions driving a live recompute rather than a static scenario$$),
  ARRAY_CONSTRUCT($$Plant locations with product capability$$, $$Customer orders with product lines and tonnage$$, $$Freight rates per km and per ton-km, plus a handling cost per ton$$, $$A provisioned routing region containing all plants and customers$$),
  ARRAY_CONSTRUCT($$Pick the cheaper fulfilment option order by order instead of applying a blanket policy$$, $$Price the handling penalty that consolidation actually costs$$, $$Give order management a defensible rule for mixed orders$$),
  $$All plant-to-customer and plant-to-plant road distances are computed live with ORS MATRIX_TABULAR (two calls), not precomputed. freight = distance_km x rate per km + tons x distance_km x rate per ton-km. Ship direct: each product line ships from its cheapest capable plant straight to the customer. Consolidate: pick a hub plant, transfer in the products the hub cannot make from the nearest capable plant (adding a handling-per-ton penalty), then ship one consolidated load from the hub. The cheaper of the two is recommended.$$,
  $$Synthetic plants, customers and orders. The model compares freight and handling only: it does not model lead time, truck availability, or the service impact of holding an order back to consolidate it. Single-line orders show near-zero savings by construction, and all plants and customers must sit inside one provisioned routing region graph.$$,
  $$query_sourcing$$,
  ARRAY_CONSTRUCT($$total consolidation savings$$, $$orders where consolidation wins$$, $$orders better shipped direct$$, $$total handling cost paid to consolidate$$),
  ARRAY_CONSTRUCT($$For customers buying several glass products, is it cheaper to consolidate at one plant or ship from several?$$, $$Which orders should we consolidate to cut freight cost?$$, $$How much handling cost do we pay to consolidate?$$),
  $$Product Mix Sourcing (Location domain, view id: mix_sourcing).
Headline: For a multi-product order, decide between shipping direct from several plants or consolidating into one truckload.
Business question: For customers buying several products, is it cheaper to ship from each plant or to transfer everything into one hub and send a single load?
Audience: Supply chain planner; Logistics manager; Order management; CFO.
Industries: Building materials; Glass and packaging; Chemicals; Industrial distribution.
Talk track: Read the header: total consolidation savings, orders where consolidation wins, orders better shipped direct, and the handling cost paid to consolidate.; Open an order where consolidation wins and walk the two options in the drawer.; Show the map: the transfer legs into the hub, then one outbound load.; Raise the handling slider until consolidation stops winning - that boundary is the customer's real decision rule.; Point out a single-line order showing near-zero saving, because there is nothing to consolidate..
Snowflake capabilities: Two live ORS MATRIX_TABULAR calls covering plant-to-customer and plant-to-plant road distance, computed on demand; Two complete fulfilment options costed per order in SQL; Interactive rate and handling assumptions driving a live recompute rather than a static scenario.
Data required: Plant locations with product capability; Customer orders with product lines and tonnage; Freight rates per km and per ton-km, plus a handling cost per ton; A provisioned routing region containing all plants and customers.
Value drivers: Pick the cheaper fulfilment option order by order instead of applying a blanket policy; Price the handling penalty that consolidation actually costs; Give order management a defensible rule for mixed orders.
Method: All plant-to-customer and plant-to-plant road distances are computed live with ORS MATRIX_TABULAR (two calls), not precomputed. freight = distance_km x rate per km + tons x distance_km x rate per ton-km. Ship direct: each product line ships from its cheapest capable plant straight to the customer. Consolidate: pick a hub plant, transfer in the products the hub cannot make from the nearest capable plant (adding a handling-per-ton penalty), then ship one consolidated load from the hub. The cheaper of the two is recommended.
Caveats: Synthetic plants, customers and orders. The model compares freight and handling only: it does not model lead time, truck availability, or the service impact of holding an order back to consolidate it. Single-line orders show near-zero savings by construction, and all plants and customers must sit inside one provisioned routing region graph.
Key metrics on screen: total consolidation savings; orders where consolidation wins; orders better shipped direct; total handling cost paid to consolidate.
Example questions: For customers buying several glass products, is it cheaper to consolidate at one plant or ship from several? Which orders should we consolidate to cut freight cost? How much handling cost do we pay to consolidate?
Gotchas: Consolidation adds a handling-per-ton penalty on every transferred product, so it only wins when the saved outbound freight beats that penalty. Single-line orders show ~0 savings. Costs use live road distance from ORS. The semantic view (query_sourcing) reports current customers/products/spend only; this consolidate-vs-direct tradeoff is computed live on this page.
Preferred analytics tool: query_sourcing.$$
