Headline: Cannibalisation what-if: how much of a new site's revenue is genuinely new, and how much is taken from your own estate.
Business question: If we open here, how much of the revenue is incremental and how much comes out of our existing stores?
Audience: Head of real estate; CFO and finance business partner; Network strategy; Franchise development.
Industries: Retail; Quick-service restaurants; Banking branch networks; Fitness and leisure.
Talk track: Pick a candidate site and a drive-time band.; Read cannibalised revenue and EBITDA, shared households, and how many existing stores are affected.; Move the three sliders - value per household, EBITDA margin, capture rate - and show the finance recompute live. These are the customer's assumptions, not ours.; Open the transfers table to name which existing store loses the most.; Switch to the overlap view to show the computed intersection geometry of the catchments.; Widen the band and show cannibalisation grow, which is the trade-off a property committee actually argues about..
Snowflake capabilities: Live drive-time isochrones intersected with ST_INTERSECTION in SQL, so overlap is computed geometry rather than stacked polygons; Household attribution by H3 cell containment at scale; A what-if model whose finance assumptions are on-screen inputs and whose recompute is a live query.
Data required: Existing store estate with locations; Candidate site locations; Household or address density (Overture works out of the box; first-party customer data is better); Your own value per household, capture rate and EBITDA margin.
Value drivers: Avoid opening a site that mostly moves existing revenue around; Defend or challenge a site proposal with a transparent, adjustable model; Rank a candidate pipeline on net rather than gross opportunity.
Method: Overlap geometry is the computed ST_INTERSECTION of live drive-time isochrones, not visual polygon stacking. Postcodes (ZIPs) are attributed by ZIP centroid containment and households by H3 cell-centroid containment. Cannibalised revenue and EBITDA are derived from your value-per-household, capture-rate and EBITDA-margin inputs. Overlap rows are pairwise (candidate versus one existing store), so do not sum households across overlap rows, because neighbouring store catchments themselves overlap; the headline KPI cards and the transfer table use non-overlapping nearest-store attribution.
Caveats: Household counts are real address density, but revenue and EBITDA are derived entirely from the assumptions set on screen, so present them as a model rather than a measurement. Drive-time bands are nested what-if scenarios: never sum transfer across bands for the same candidate and store pair. The store estate and its interaction mix are synthetic for the demo.
Key metrics on screen: cannibalised revenue and EBITDA; shared households; existing stores impacted.
Example questions: How much would the candidate site cannibalise within a 20-minute drive? Which existing store loses the most revenue to the new site?
Gotchas: Drive-time bands are nested what-if scenarios; never sum transfer across bands for the same candidate and store pair. Household counts are real (address density); revenue and EBITDA are derived from the user-supplied value per household, capture rate and EBITDA margin, and the Home Visit / Sample / Walk-in split percentages come from the store estate.
Preferred analytics tool: query_location.$$
