  ARRAY_CONSTRUCT($$current annual freight spend$$, $$optimized annual freight spend$$, $$total annual savings$$, $$customers with a cheaper source$$),
  ARRAY_CONSTRUCT($$Based on our plant locations, where would location swaps reduce freight costs?$$, $$Which customer has the biggest freight saving from switching source plant?$$, $$How much can we save in total by re-sourcing?$$),
  $$Freight Sourcing Optimizer (Location domain, view id: sourcing_optimizer).
Headline: Serve each customer from the cheapest plant that can actually make their product, priced on real road distance.
Business question: Where are we shipping from the wrong plant, and what would re-sourcing save us in a year?
Audience: Supply chain director; Logistics and freight manager; CFO; Commercial operations.
Industries: Building materials; Glass and packaging; Chemicals; Food and beverage manufacturing.
Talk track: Read the header: current annual freight spend, optimized spend and the total saving.; Show the swaps table sorted by saving and open the biggest one.; Put it on the map: current source plant versus proposed plant for that customer.; Move the per-km and per-ton-km sliders to the customer's real rates and watch the saving move.; Stress that the distance matrix is one live routing call over the road network, not straight lines..
Snowflake capabilities: A single live ORS MATRIX_TABULAR call producing an all-plants by all-customers road-distance matrix at interaction time; A table function that turns that matrix into rows and picks the winning source per customer in SQL; Product capability constraints enforced in the same query as the cost model.
Data required: Plant locations with product capability; Customer locations with product and annual volume; Freight rates per km and per ton-km; A provisioned routing region containing all plants and customers.
Value drivers: Cut freight spend with no capital investment - it is a sourcing decision, not a build; Quantify a re-sourcing business case customer by customer; Expose where straight-line planning has been quietly wrong.
Method: The plant-to-customer road-distance matrix is computed live with a single ORS MATRIX_TABULAR call (all plants by all customers), not precomputed. Freight cost per truckload = distance_km x rate per km + tons x distance_km x rate per ton-km. The current source is a data-only baseline (the nearest product-capable plant by straight-line distance); the optimizer picks the cheapest source by actual road distance, so the gap is the achievable saving. Only plants that can make the customer's product are considered, and annual savings are the per-load saving multiplied by the customer's annual truckloads.
Caveats: Plants, customers and volumes are synthetic. The saving is only as good as the rates entered, and it assumes the proposed plant has the capacity to absorb the volume - capacity is not modelled. All plants and customers must sit inside one provisioned routing region graph.
Key metrics on screen: current annual freight spend; optimized annual freight spend; total annual savings; customers with a cheaper source.
Example questions: Based on our plant locations, where would location swaps reduce freight costs? Which customer has the biggest freight saving from switching source plant? How much can we save in total by re-sourcing?
Gotchas: Savings only consider plants that can make the customer's product. Freight cost is per truckload and uses live road distance from ORS; the current-source baseline is the nearest capable plant by straight-line distance. Annual savings = per-load saving x the customer's annual truckloads. The semantic view (query_sourcing) reports current estate/spend only; the swap recommendation is computed live on this page.
Preferred analytics tool: query_sourcing.$$
