  $$Chain skeletons are enumerated in SQL: first hops are loads that carry the vehicle CLOSER to its target (a first hop that already arrives is a direct return and is excluded), kept only when progress toward the target at least pays for the empty km spent reaching it, and bounded to the best few per vehicle before a second hop is joined on. Hop ordering is enforced by an estimated first-leg delivery time because the solver has no precedence constraint. Surviving chains are then costed on the live road network with a single matrix call, scored on utilisation, how completely they close the return, and empty km saved, and filtered by an internal-first cascade that stops at the first rung meeting the acceptance score.$$,
  $$Vehicles, loads and offers are synthetic, and revenue per loaded km and cost per empty km are stated assumptions rather than source data. Vehicle equipment is synthesized and the equipment constraint is seeded off. Chaining only applies over long distances: on a single-metro dataset the page correctly reports that no chain is needed. Read-only - nothing is written back. Requires the region routing services to be running.$$,
  $$$$,
  ARRAY_CONSTRUCT($$chains (costed two-hop candidates), vehicles covered, and the cascade rung the ladder stopped at (1 own-loads-only .. 4 external-on-both-hops)$$, $$per chain: both hops as pickup -> delivery with own/external on each, empty km, loaded km, remaining distance to target, grade and eligibility$$, $$status-quo comparison per chain: chain empty km against the empty run home, chain net against doing nothing, and whether the chain beats that baseline$$, $$the on-screen chains are published as __memo_triangle_proposals (trailer, rung, both hops, empty and loaded km, net vs baseline, beats-baseline, grade)$$),
  ARRAY_CONSTRUCT($$which vehicles have a two-hop chain home?$$, $$did we need to go to an external exchange for any of these chains?$$, $$which chains do not beat simply running home empty?$$, $$why is there no chain for this vehicle?$$, $$how much empty running does the best chain save?$$),
  $$Triangle Proposals (Optimization domain, view id: triangle_proposals).
Headline: Recovers return trips that single-hop matching cannot see, by chaining two loads through an intermediate drop and pricing the chain against the cost of running home empty.
Business question: There is no load from here back to where this vehicle is needed. Is there a pair of loads that gets it home via an intermediate stop, and is that chain actually better than running empty?
Audience: Head of transport; Dispatch supervisor; Network optimization lead; Freight buyer.
Industries: Road freight and haulage; Trailer and container operators; Retail and grocery distribution; Third-party logistics.
Talk track: Start with the gap this fills: single-hop matching asks 'is there a load from here to my target'. A chain asks 'is there a load that gets me CLOSER', which is a far larger candidate set.; Show the cascade rung on a card. Rung 1 means both hops came from our own loads and the external exchange was never queried - that ordering is a policy, not a scoring nudge.; Press Cost legs on road network and point out that one matrix call prices every leg of every candidate chain live, with nothing precomputed.; Walk the status-quo line on a card: chain empty km against the empty run home, and net against doing nothing. This is the number a planner argues with.; Show a chain flagged as not beating the baseline and make the point that the tool says so rather than presenting it as a win.; Read the constraint chips: total empty km, first-hop detour, reaches target, hop order. Hop order is enforced structurally so a chain can never be delivered out of sequence.; If the active dataset is a single metro area, explain that the page correctly reports no chain is needed, because every load already delivers within the target radius. Chaining is a long-haul pattern..
Snowflake capabilities: Chain enumeration as a bounded load-to-load self-join in SQL over the neutral contract views, so the candidate set is explainable rather than a black box; One live travel-time matrix call against the routing engine on Snowpark Container Services prices every leg of every candidate chain at interaction time; The outside-load lookup sits behind a contract table function, so a real freight-exchange integration replaces one function body with no change to this page.
Data required: Vehicle locations with a forward-looking availability time and a return target; Own waiting loads and external offers with pickup windows, weight and geography; Revenue per loaded km and cost per empty km; A provisioned routing region covering the lanes in the chain.
Value drivers: Return trips recovered that a direct-match search structurally cannot find; Own capacity used before money is spent on an outside exchange; A defensible comparison against running empty, which is what earns planner trust.
Method: Chain skeletons are enumerated in SQL: first hops are loads that carry the vehicle CLOSER to its target (a first hop that already arrives is a direct return and is excluded), kept only when progress toward the target at least pays for the empty km spent reaching it, and bounded to the best few per vehicle before a second hop is joined on. Hop ordering is enforced by an estimated first-leg delivery time because the solver has no precedence constraint. Surviving chains are then costed on the live road network with a single matrix call, scored on utilisation, how completely they close the return, and empty km saved, and filtered by an internal-first cascade that stops at the first rung meeting the acceptance score.
Caveats: Vehicles, loads and offers are synthetic, and revenue per loaded km and cost per empty km are stated assumptions rather than source data. Vehicle equipment is synthesized and the equipment constraint is seeded off. Chaining only applies over long distances: on a single-metro dataset the page correctly reports that no chain is needed. Read-only - nothing is written back. Requires the region routing services to be running.
Key metrics on screen: chains (costed two-hop candidates), vehicles covered, and the cascade rung the ladder stopped at (1 own-loads-only .. 4 external-on-both-hops); per chain: both hops as pickup -> delivery with own/external on each, empty km, loaded km, remaining distance to target, grade and eligibility; status-quo comparison per chain: chain empty km against the empty run home, chain net against doing nothing, and whether the chain beats that baseline; the on-screen chains are published as __memo_triangle_proposals (trailer, rung, both hops, empty and loaded km, net vs baseline, beats-baseline, grade).
Example questions: which vehicles have a two-hop chain home? did we need to go to an external exchange for any of these chains? which chains do not beat simply running home empty? why is there no chain for this vehicle? how much empty running does the best chain save?$$
